('75% noticed shrinkflation, 48% switched brands; shrinkflation drove 3-10% of grocery inflation 2019-2023 on top of CPI.','75% of Americans have\nnoticed shrinkflation','Not fetched. The "on top of the CPI increases" framing is doubtful: BLS CPI adjusts for package-size changes, so shrinkflation is largely captured in the headline index. '+U,[]),
('About 30% of US footwear comes from Vietnam; on 2 April 2025 a 10% baseline tariff and a 46% Vietnam reciprocal rate were announced.','Vietnam got hit with a 46% reciprocal tariff','Liberation Day 10% baseline and 46% Vietnam rate '+R+'; Vietnam\'s ~30% footwear share also by recall. The 46% rate was paused before taking effect and later replaced by a 20% deal rate, so "suddenly had a 46% levy" overstates what was collected.',[]),
('Footwear prices rose ~40% short-run, clothing 33-37%, new cars +$7,600, food +4.5% from tariffs.','Footwear prices jumped roughly 40% in\nthe short run','These resemble Yale Budget Lab short-run price-effect estimates in later 2025 updates (model projections, not observed price changes); the video presents them as realised jumps. Exact values not checked. '+U,[]),
('Yale Budget Lab: peak household cost $3,800/year, settling to $1,700-$2,400.','Yale\'s Budget Lab estimated the peak\ncost','YBL verified: April 2025 estimate ~$3,800 per household; November 2025 update ~$1,700.',['YBL']),
('The Tax Foundation called the peak tariff rate 22.5%, the highest since 1909.','The Tax Foundation called the\npeak tariff rate 22.5%','YBL: the 22.5% "highest since 1909" figure is Yale Budget Lab\'s (April 2025), not the Tax Foundation\'s. MISATTRIBUTED. YBL\'s April 2026 update put the rate at 11.0%, so the peak is stale as a current figure.',['YBL']),
('A New York Fed analysis found Americans bore 94% of tariff costs.','A New York Fed analysis found that\nAmericans bore 94%','NYF26 verified with scope: 94% applies to Jan-Aug 2025 import-price pass-through; it fell to 92% (Sep-Oct) and 86% (Nov), about 90% for the year. Presented without the declining trend.',['NYF26']),
('Credit card debt hit $1.277 trillion, the highest since records began in 1999, up 66% from the pandemic low.','American\ncredit card debt just hit $1.277','NYF4Q verified approximately: NY Fed Q4 2025 card balances $1.28 trillion (record; series starts 1999). The 66% rise not checked.',['NYF4Q']),
('Americans paid $160 [billion] in card interest in 2024, about $480 per person.','Americans paid\n$160\nin credit card interest','ASR drops "billion". K02: 160e9 / 340M = $471 (reproduces approximately). The $160 billion total is not fetched. '+U,[]),
('Average balance $6,618 at 22.3% APR with minimum payments takes over 18 years and costs $9,259 interest.','owes\n$6,618','K02: the result depends on the minimum-payment formula. 3%-of-balance minimum gives 19.4 years and $9,894 interest; interest + 1% gives 21.6 years and $11,224. The direction holds and the exact figures are not reproduced. Balance and APR not fetched.',[]),
('Isabella: $8,000 at 22% costs ~$147/month in interest, $1,764/year.','Isabella\'s $8,000 in credit card debt','K02: 8,000 x 22% / 12 = $146.67; 147 x 12 = $1,764. Reproduces. Composite character.',[]),
('Fed data: 73% of balances come from essentials; 55% carry balances for necessities.','73% of credit card\nbalances','Not fetched; attributed to "Federal Reserve data" without a locator. '+U,[]),
('12.7% of all credit card balances are 90+ days delinquent.','12.7% of all credit card\nbalances','NYF4Q: the NY Fed reports an annualised flow into serious delinquency of about 7.1% for cards in Q4 2025. The 12.7% figure was not found and may be a different stock measure or a misquote. Unsupported as stated.',['NYF4Q']),
('New car payment averaged $767 in late 2025 (record); nearly 1 in 5 loans over $1,000; 84-month loans 21.5%, nearly triple 2015.','The average new car\npayment hit $767','ED4Q: Edmunds Q4 2025 average $772; more than 1 in 5 committed to $1,000+; 84+ month loans 20.8%. The video\'s values are close but do not match the Q4 release. The "triple 2015" share is not checked.',['ED4Q']),
('29.4% of new car buyers roll negative equity, $7,064 on average.','29.4%\nof new car buyers','ED4Q: Q4 2025 29.3% of trade-ins with negative equity, averaging $7,214 (record). Approximately right but not matching. The share applies to trade-ins, not all buyers.',['ED4Q']),
('Student loans $1.696 trillion, 43 million borrowers, average $39,633; serious-delinquency transition 14.3%, fastest since 2000; 7 million in default; SAVE ruled unlawful, 7.5 million transition by July 2026.','Then, there are student\nloans, $1.696','The SAVE plan court loss and wind-down '+R+'. The total, borrower count, average and delinquency figures are not checked. '+U,[]),
('Pandemic excess savings peaked at $2.1 trillion (Aug 2021) and were depleted by March 2024 (SF Fed).','accumulated $2.1 trillion','SFF verified: SF Fed estimated peak $2.1T in August 2021, fully depleted by March 2024. Model-dependent; ABN AMRO estimated ~$1.6T remaining.',['SFF']),
('Saving rate 4% vs 8.4% historical average; 37% cannot cover $400 with cash; 59% cannot handle $1,000.','37% of Americans cannot cover a $400','The 37% matches the Fed SHED 2024 (63% would cover with cash), and 59% matches Bankrate 2025, both '+R+'. Saving rate not checked.',[]),
